{
  "requirement": "Saved configuration MUST remain distinct from resolved runtime configuration.\nSerializing and deserializing a valid configuration MUST preserve its semantic\nvalues, including explicit versus omitted settings. Optional fields MAY be\nomitted on serialization; defaults and environment-derived values MUST NOT be\nmaterialized into omitted fields. TOML comments and formatting need not\nround-trip.\n\nLoading configuration or running a configured feature MUST NOT rewrite the user\nor project configuration as a side effect. Resolved credentials and live runtime\nobjects MUST NOT enter the serialized data structures. Explicitly supplied\ncredential values are permitted only in the fields documented by the authoring\nfeature; credentials resolved from a profile or environment MUST NOT be written\nback to configuration.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the sources satisfy `requirements.configuration.serialization`.**\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- **Round trip and omitted settings.** `UserConfig::parse`/`to_toml` and `WorkspaceConfig::parse`/`to_toml` (src/config.rs:253-316) are marked `{impl}`.\n  - They decode through serde with `deny_unknown_fields` and re-validate before and after.\n  - The serialized types (`UserConfig`, `WorkspaceConfig`, `WorkspaceAuthoringConfig`, `UserAuthoringConfig`, `AuthoringConfig`, `BedrockConfig`, `OllamaConfig`, `EvidenceConfig`) use `#[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = ...)]` on optional fields.\n  - Omitted `endpoint`, `concurrency`, `region`, `aws_profile`, `credentials` and `api_key` stay `None` and are not written back.\n  - A present-but-empty section such as `[authoring]` or `[evidence]` is still written as a table, so presence is preserved.\n  - `UserAuthoringConfig.configs` is required and always written, so an explicit `configs = {}` round-trips.\n  - Empty `options`, `projects` and workspace `configs` are dropped on output. `requirements.authoring.configuration_serialization` says omitted tables deserialize as empty maps, so the semantic values are unchanged.\n- **Defaults and environment values are not materialized.**\n  - The `endpoint()` accessor applies the default at use time.\n  - `effective_concurrency()` applies its default at use time. It lives on `ResolvedAuthoringConfig`, which is not serializable.\n  - `OllamaConfig::api_key_from` (marked `{impl}`) returns a `String` read from the environment and never stores it in a config struct.\n  - `ResolvedAuthoringConfig` derives no `Serialize`, and `resolve_authoring_with_workspace_from` (marked `{impl}`) only copies saved values.\n- **No rewriting as a side effect.** `load_user_config`, `load_workspace_config` and `resolve_authoring*` are marked `{impl}` and only read. A search of `src` found no code that calls `to_toml` or writes either configuration file. The only file writes are evidence and usage records, `book.toml` setup, and the like, none of which touch these files.\n- **Credentials.** The only places a credential value can be serialized are `credentials.*` and `api_key`, through `SecretString`. Those are the fields the authoring credentials feature documents. Credentials resolved from a profile or environment have no field in the serialized structs.\n- **Marking gap, judged minor.** The serde attributes on the structs are marked only with `authoring.configuration_serialization`, `structure` and `feature_sections`, not this requirement. That requirement cites this one for its common rules, and its text carries out the authoring part. `EvidenceConfig` and `SecretString` carry similar attributes. The omission rules are MAY-level, and the MUST obligations are marked at `parse`, `to_toml`, the loaders and the resolvers. I do not count this as a gap.\n\n**Tests** (tests/configuration.rs, all marked `{test}`)\n\n- `absent_and_minimal_configuration_are_distinct_and_read_only`: an absent file stays absent, a minimal file keeps its bytes and comment after loading, and the config round-trips.\n- `backend_configuration_round_trips_without_materializing_defaults`: omitted `endpoint` and `concurrency` are absent from the output, nothing like `http://localhost` is written, and the round trip is equal.\n- `authoring_concurrency_defaults_overrides_and_invalid_values`: the default is not written, an explicit `concurrency = 7` is, and invalid values are rejected.\n- `ollama_authentication_resolves_separately_and_rejects_missing_values`: the env-resolved key `private-value` never appears in the serialization, while the `MODEL_KEY` name does.\n- `workspace_discovery_uses_nearest_file_without_merging_or_rewriting_it`: the file bytes are unchanged after loading, and the workspace round trip excludes `configs`.\n- `explicit_credentials_round_trip_but_diagnostics_and_debug_redact_them`: explicit credentials round-trip, and malformed, conflicting or empty credential inputs are rejected.\n- `user_and_workspace_authoring_tables_round_trip_every_map`: `projects`, an empty `configs`, and populated `configs` with `options` all round-trip.\n- `workspace_defaults_and_configs_combine_with_existing_user_selections`: resolution combines user and workspace selections.\n\nTogether they cover the explicit-versus-omitted boundary, the no-rewrite property, credential separation, and failure cases.\n\n**Remaining observations (not gaps):** no test hashes the file after a full `resolve_authoring` call. The code has no write path, so that is acceptable. No FIXME is needed.",
  "references": "8568e11156882e5f9fa3ff681455163831d68d1a4f6816b022e994dbd51f1dea",
